# Local Docker Compose Postgres (see docker-compose.yml):
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://bulbau:bulbau_dev_only@localhost:5432/bulbau
#
# Or a personal Neon dev branch (recommended — see docs/TECHSPEC.md §10.1).
# For LOCAL dev use the DIRECT (unpooled) connection string — the hostname does
# NOT contain "-pooler" — because local dev runs Payload in push mode, which
# issues DDL, and Neon's PgBouncer pooler (transaction pooling) breaks schema
# changes. NEVER point local dev at the production branch (a `npm run dev` would
# push schema straight into production).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@ep-example.eu-central-1.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require
#
# The POOLED URL (hostname WITH "-pooler") is what a DEPLOYED stage's runtime
# uses — set as the SST DatabaseUrl secret — not local dev. Deployed-stage
# migrations (`npm run migrate`) always use the DIRECT/unpooled URL; CI reads it
# from the STAGING_/PRODUCTION_DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED GitHub secrets. See README
# "Database migrations".

# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
PAYLOAD_SECRET=YOUR_SECRET_HERE

# TOTP (2FA) secret encryption + step-up cookie signing — a separate secret
# from PAYLOAD_SECRET on purpose (key separation). Generate the same way:
# openssl rand -base64 32
TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=YOUR_TOTP_KEY_HERE

# Rate limiting / lockout for TOTP verification attempts (Upstash Redis —
# see docs/TECHSPEC.md §3). Required in staging/production. If left unset,
# src/lib/totp/rateLimit.ts falls back to an in-memory limiter — fine for
# local dev and CI (same idea as S3Mock standing in for real S3 below), but
# NOT safe for a real multi-instance deployment. Create a free database at
# https://console.upstash.com/ and copy its REST URL/token:
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=https://xxxxx.upstash.io
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=YOUR_UPSTASH_TOKEN

# Media uploads use @payloadcms/storage-s3 — there is no local-disk fallback
# (Lambda's filesystem is read-only outside /tmp, so local-disk storage was
# never an option). Point S3_BUCKET at any bucket you can reach (a personal
# AWS bucket, or a bucket in a shared dev account) for real-AWS local dev:
S3_BUCKET=your-dev-bucket-name
AWS_REGION=eu-central-1

# Or, to avoid needing real AWS credentials at all for local dev, run the
# S3Mock container (see docker-compose.yml) and point at it instead — leave
# S3_BUCKET as below to match its pre-created bucket:
# S3_BUCKET=bulbau-dev
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9090
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test

# ── Public site (Phase 2) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical origin used for canonical tags, hreflang alternates, Open Graph URLs
# and the sitemap (src/lib/seo.ts). Locally you can leave this unset (defaults to
# the production domain). On a deployed stage it's an SST secret (SiteUrl); set it
# to that stage's real origin (e.g. the CloudFront URL on staging) for accurate
# canonicals. NEXT_PUBLIC_* ⇒ inlined into the build.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
#
# Search-engine indexing gate. OFF unless explicitly 'true'. Leave unset/empty
# everywhere except the production stage at launch — this is what keeps the
# staging CloudFront URL out of search results (SST secret: AllowIndexing).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING=false

# ── Translation (Phase 5) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AWS Translate auto-translation of CMS content (EN → FR/DE on save). OFF unless
# set to 'true'. When unset the translation hook (src/lib/translation/*) is a
# no-op and FR/DE fall back to the EN source via Payload's localization fallback —
# so local dev and CI need no AWS Translate access. On deployed stages SST sets
# this to 'true' automatically (the Lambda role carries translate:TranslateText —
# no API key or secret). To exercise it in LOCAL dev, set it to 'true' AND provide
# AWS credentials allowed to call translate:TranslateText (e.g. an AWS profile via
# AWS_PROFILE / standard AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID env) — the fake S3Mock creds above are
# NOT sufficient. Easiest is to verify on staging instead.
# TRANSLATE_ENABLED=true

# ── CDN invalidation (CloudFront) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# On a content change, the revalidate hook (src/lib/revalidate.ts) invalidates the
# public CloudFront distribution so the edit appears at the edge within seconds
# (OpenNext does not do this by default). SST sets this AUTOMATICALLY on deployed
# stages to the NAME of the SSM parameter that holds the distribution id — it is
# NOT set by hand. When unset (local dev / CI), src/lib/cdn/invalidate.ts is a
# no-op and the time-based ISR window handles freshness, so no AWS access is
# needed locally. Shown here for reference only:
# CDN_DISTRIBUTION_ID_PARAM=/bulbau-lu/<stage>/web-cdn-distribution-id

# ── Email delivery — SES (Phase 4 part 2) ────────────────────────────────────
# Verified SES "From" address for the "email me the quote" action
# (src/lib/email/*). When UNSET the send path is a no-op that returns
# not_configured and the UI offers the PDF download instead — so local dev and CI
# need no SES access (same env-gated pattern as TRANSLATE_ENABLED / the CDN param
# above). On a deployed stage it's an SST secret (EmailSender); set it only once
# the SES DOMAIN IDENTITY on bulbau.lu is verified (DKIM/SPF/DMARC applied via
# infra/terraform/ses.tf) and — for sending to arbitrary visitor addresses — SES
# production access has been granted (the sandbox only delivers to verified
# recipients). To exercise it in LOCAL dev you would also need AWS credentials
# allowed to call ses:SendEmail; easiest is to verify on staging instead.
# EMAIL_SENDER=info@bulbau.lu

# ── Spam protection — Cloudflare Turnstile (Phase 6) ─────────────────────────
# Free, privacy-friendly CAPTCHA alternative for the contact form and the
# "email me the quote" action (src/lib/security/turnstile.ts + the forms). BOTH
# OPTIONAL: when unset the server-side check is a no-op that PASSES and the client
# widget does not render — so local dev / CI need no Cloudflare account (same
# env-gated pattern as EMAIL_SENDER above). On a deployed stage they are SST
# secrets (TurnstileSecretKey / TurnstileSiteKey). Create a widget in the
# Cloudflare dashboard (Turnstile → Add site, hostname bulbau.lu) to get the pair;
# the SECRET key must stay server-side, the SITE key is public.
#
# For LOCAL/automated testing you can use Cloudflare's always-pass dummy keys
# (they work on any domain incl. localhost and are safe to commit):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA   # always passes
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA  # always passes
# Real keys look nothing like these. Production keys reject the dummy token and
# vice-versa. Leave both UNSET to skip Turnstile entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
